# plans/board-workflows-2026-09-20-phase-8-plan.md P8-T05 · UX-SPEC.md §Board settings ·
# KEYMAP.md §Dialog > BoardSettings · BOARD.md §11.10 · TESTING-HARNESS.md §3
# (`settings.columns`) and §11 (the `section` and row-editor fields) — `C` opens Board settings
# on Columns, and a column's automation edited there survives one `^s` and a re-opening.
#
# The round trip is the point: the dialog is a *draft* until `^s`, and `settings.columns` is
# projected from the board rather than from that draft (`state/harness/projection.rs`), so every
# assertion below about the list is a statement about what the daemon actually stored. Three
# things ride on that:
#   `P`          the preset is idempotent. This board already carries every workflow column, so
#                `P` must add none — and the proof is not the list while the dialog is open (it
#                cannot move before a save) but the list after `^s` has written the whole column
#                vector back: a `P` that duplicated a column would show it there.
#   the `effort` the one row-scoped editor Board settings mounts is the only place a value typed
#   field       into this dialog can be read back (§11); every other row is a cycler whose value
#                `settings.columns` or the board already carries. It is named after its row, so
#                asserting `fields[1].name == "effort"` also pins *which* row the walk landed on.
#   `⏎` twice    in the Columns pane `⏎` drills into a column, and only inside the form does it
#                open a row's editor — which is what leaves `n`, `d`, `J`/`K` and `P` meaning
#                themselves in the list. The two key contexts are the proof: `BoardSettings`
#                while the list owns the keys, `BoardSettingsEditing` while the editor does.
#
# `blistering` is an effort no catalogue offers, and nothing validates one (contracts §5.3), so
# it cannot be confused with a value the column already carried or a default the app supplied.
#
# The fields are read with `assert`, never `await`: typing does not notify `AppState`, and only
# `assert` and `dump` project on demand (§11). Everything that crosses the daemon — the save,
# the re-opening — is awaited.
#
# No `shot`: this is a keyboard scenario, so it runs in the headless lane too (§7).
fixture: board-workflow
await idle 30000

# The preset seeds two worktrees, and `agent` is the one carrying the board.
assert lists.worktrees.rows[1].id == "acme/api#agent"
key j
await lists.worktrees.selected.id == "acme/api#agent" 10000
key o
await screen == Workspace 30000
key ctrl-s b
await lists.tabs.selected.label == "board" 30000
await focused == "board.column[0].card[0]" 30000
await key_contexts[2] == "Board" 20000

# `C` opens the dialog on Columns whatever section it was last left on, and the rail section is
# the one piece of draft state a projection cannot otherwise reach.
key shift-c
await dialog.name == BoardSettings 15000
assert dialog.fields[0].name == "section" && dialog.fields[0].value == "Columns"
assert key_contexts[0] == "Dialog" && key_contexts[1] == "BoardSettings"

# The list is this board's own columns in board order, and the two that start a run on arrival
# carry the same `action` mark the board face draws. Nothing is disabled: a worktree board on
# the local backend is exactly the board that may carry automation.
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[0].label == "Backlog" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[0].marks[0] absent
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[3].label == "In Progress" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[3].marks[0] == "action"
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[4].label == "In review" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[4].marks[0] == "action"
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[6].label == "Canceled" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[7] absent

# `P` on a board that already has every preset column: the draft must come out of it unchanged,
# which the save below is what proves.
key shift-p

# Four rows down to In review, then into its form. The list keys stop meaning themselves here,
# which is why the drill-in is a mode rather than a selection.
key j j j j
key enter

# Name, Category, On enter, Provider, Model, Effort — the sixth row of a column that runs
# something. `⏎` opens its editor and the key context says the editor owns the keyboard.
key j j j j j
key enter
assert key_contexts[1] == "BoardSettingsEditing"
assert dialog.fields[1].name == "effort"
type blistering
assert dialog.fields[1].value == "blistering"

# `⏎` commits the editor back into the draft and the list takes the keyboard again.
key enter
assert key_contexts[1] == "BoardSettings"

# One `^s` is the whole save: `UpdateBoard` carries every column, and the dialog closes only
# once the daemon has answered it.
key ctrl-s
await dialog absent 30000
await idle 30000

# Re-opened on the board the daemon wrote back. The column vector is the one `P` was pressed on,
# so a preset that had added a duplicate would be showing it here.
key shift-c
await dialog.name == BoardSettings 15000
assert dialog.fields[0].value == "Columns"
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[0].label == "Backlog"
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[3].label == "In Progress" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[3].marks[0] == "action"
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[4].label == "In review" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[4].marks[0] == "action"
assert lists["settings.columns"].rows[6].label == "Canceled" && lists["settings.columns"].rows[7] absent

# The same walk to the same row, over a draft seeded from the stored board: the effort is what
# was typed before the save.
key j j j j
key enter
key j j j j j
key enter
assert key_contexts[1] == "BoardSettingsEditing"
assert dialog.fields[1].name == "effort" && dialog.fields[1].value == "blistering"
dump columns

# `esc` climbs out one level at a time: the editor, then the column, then the dialog — and a
# draft nothing has changed since the save closes without asking.
key escape
assert key_contexts[1] == "BoardSettings"
key escape
key escape
await dialog absent 10000
quit
